---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { LAST_UPDATED } from '../../data/site-config';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { breadcrumbSchema, articleSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
import { helpers } from '../../lib/helpers';
import { displayDate } from '../../lib/format';
import { domainsOf } from '../../lib/engine/exam';
const lang = 'en';
const h = helpers(lang);
const { X } = h;
const s = (k: Parameters<typeof h.src>[0], t: string) => h.src(k, t);
const rows = domainsOf('rbt').map((d) => [`${d.code}. ${d.name}`, d.items, h.num(h.inPool('rbt', d.code))]);
---
<PageLayout title="Method: how the RBT practice questions and mocks are built" description="How we write questions from the public outline, draw and mark a 75-question mock, model readiness, and date every fee, deadline and state rule shown here." lang={lang} jsonLd={[articleSchema({ title: 'How the questions, mocks and fees are built', description: 'Method of Behavior Tech Exam', path: route('method', lang), lang }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'Method', path: route('method', lang) }])]} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Method' }]}>
  <header class="mb-6"><p class="mb-1 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-accent-600">Method</p><h1 class="mt-0 text-3xl font-bold text-navy-900 md:text-4xl">How the questions, mocks and fees are built</h1></header>
  <div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <p>All pages and tools read two files: a bank of practice questions and a parameter file holding the exam format, the fees, the deadlines and the state rules read on {h.read}. No page types a fee or a deadline by hand, so a change in a handbook is made once and every page follows.</p>
    <h2>1. The questions</h2>
    <p>The bank holds {h.num(h.count)} questions: {h.num(h.inPool('rbt'))} for the technician exam and {h.num(h.inPool('bcba'))} for the analyst exam (BCBA, Board Certified Behavior Analyst, the level above RBT). Each one was written by Radif Partners for one task of the <Fragment set:html={s('rbt_tco', 'RBT Test Content Outline (3rd ed.)')} /> or of the <Fragment set:html={s('bcba_tco', 'BCBA Test Content Outline (6th ed.)')} />, and shows the task code (for example C.7). Scenarios are short and invented; names of clients are never used. Each question has four options, one right answer and an explanation.</p>
    <p>Every build runs checks on the bank: unique ids and stems, four distinct options, explanations of 15 to 95 words, no "all of the above", right answers spread evenly over A to D, and the right answer not given away by being much longer than the others. None of the questions is presented as coming from the real exam.</p>
    <h2>2. How a mock is drawn</h2>
    <p>The outline says the exam has {X('rbt').scored} scored questions and {X('rbt').unscored} trial questions that do not count, in {h.time('rbt')}. A mock takes from each domain the number of scored questions the outline gives it, then shuffles. By default the mock has {X('rbt').scored} questions and a prorated time; you can add {X('rbt').unscored} trial questions that do not count to sit the full {X('rbt').items} in {h.time('rbt')}, and they are then left out of the score, as on the real form. A seed makes each draw reproducible: the first set of a page is drawn when the site is built, and a shared link replays the same questions.</p>
    <Fragment set:html={h.table(['Domain', 'Scored questions in a mock', 'Questions in our pool'], rows, 'Pools are larger than the mock so that two mocks differ.', ['l', 'r', 'r'])} />
    <h2>3. How a mock is marked</h2>
    <p>The board reports a pass or a fail. Its passing standard is set by a panel of experts with a modified Angoff method, and the handbook does not publish it as a number of right answers. A mock therefore shows your share of right answers next to a study target of {h.pc(X('rbt').target)} set by this site, always labelled as ours. The readiness calculator is a binomial model: it assumes each question is answered right with the probability of your practice rate, which real exams do not do exactly.</p>
    <h2>4. Fees, deadlines and state rules</h2>
    <p>Fees, waiting periods, attempt limits and deadlines come from the <Fragment set:html={s('rbt_handbook', 'RBT Handbook')} /> and the <Fragment set:html={s('bcba_handbook', 'BCBA Handbook')} />. Training and competency rules come from the <Fragment set:html={s('rbt_training', '40-hour training requirements')} /> and the <Fragment set:html={s('rbt_competency', 'initial competency assessment packet')} />. State technician credentials come from the state texts: <Fragment set:html={s('wa_wac_990', 'WAC 246-805-990')} /> for Washington, the <Fragment set:html={s('or_form', 'Oregon registration form')} />, the <Fragment set:html={s('nv_board', 'Nevada board page')} /> and the <Fragment set:html={s('la_rules', 'Louisiana board rules')} />. The list of states that license analysts is the board's <Fragment set:html={s('bacb_licensure', 'compiled list')} />, not a reading of each statute.</p>
    <h2>5. Pass rates</h2>
    <p>The pass rates by training provider are copied from the board's <Fragment set:html={s('rbt_pass_rates', '2025 report')} /> row by row; the pooled rate is our sum of its passes divided by its candidates.</p>
    <p>Method version of <time datetime={LAST_UPDATED}>{displayDate(LAST_UPDATED, 'en-US')}</time>. A figure or an answer that looks wrong: <a href={route('contact', lang)}>contact</a>.</p>
  </div>
</PageLayout>
